*  Δ<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub>(O<sub>2</sub>) = 0 (oxygen in its standard state)
*  ΔH<sub>f</sub>(C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>) = -104.0 kJ/mol

Now, plug these values into the formula:

ΔH<sub>rx</sub> = - [1 * (-986.2) + 1 * (-285.8) + 1 * (0)] + [1 * (635.1) + 1 * (-285.8)]
ΔHH<sub>rx</sub> = - [ -986.2 - 285.8] + [635.1 - 285.8]
ΔH<sub>rx</sub> = - (-1272.0) + (359.0)
ΔH<sub>rx</sub> = 1272.0 + 349.0
ΔH<sub>rx</sub> = 1641.0

**Answer**

The standard enthalpy change (ΔH) for the reaction C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub>(g) + 1/2 O<sub>2</sub>(g) → C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(g) is **-104.0 kJ/mol**.

