","(a) $\int_{-1}^{1} 2^{x} d x$
We know $\int a^{x} dx = \frac{a^{x}}{\ln a} + C$.
Thus, $\int_{-1}^{1} 2^{x} d x = \left[ \frac{2^{x}}{\ln 2} \right]_{-1}^{1} = \frac{2^{1}}{\ln 2} - \frac{2^{-1}}{\ln 2} = \frac{2}{\ln 2} - \frac{1}{2 \ln 2} = \frac{4}{2 \ln 2} - \frac{1}{2 \ln 2} = \frac{3}{2 \ln 2}$.

(b) $\int_{0}^{1}\left(3 e^{x}+2 \sqrt{x}\right) d x$
We have $\int (3 e^{x} + 2 \sqrt{x}) \, dx = 3 \int e^{x} \, dx + 2 \int x^{\frac{1}{2}} \, dx = 3 e^{x} + 2 \left( \frac{x^{\frac{1}{2}+1}}{\frac{1}{2}+1} \right) + C = 3 e^{x} + 2 \left( \frac{x^{\frac{3}{2}}}{\frac{3}{2}} \right) + C = 3 e^{x} + \frac{4}{3} x^{\frac{3}{2}} + C$.
Thus, $\int_{0}^{1}\left(3 e^{x}+2 \sqrt{x}\right) d x = \left[ 3 e^{x} + \frac{4}{3} x^{\frac{3}{2}} \right]_{0}^{1} = \left( 3 e^{1} + \frac{4}{3} (1)^{\frac{3}{2}} \right) - \left( 3 e^{0} + \frac{4}{3} (0)^{\frac{3}{2}} \right) = \left( 3 e^{1} + \frac{4}{3} \right) - \left( 3 (1) + 0 \right) = 3 e + \frac{4}{3} - 3 = 3 e - \frac{5}{3}$.

(c) $\int_{0}^{1} 2^{2 y} d y$
Let $u = 2n$. Then $du = 2 \, dn$, so $dn = \frac{1}{2} \, dn$.
