","Let $f(x)$ is an even function, which means $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $1-x$.
Since $f(x)$ is an even function and is increasing for $x \in [0, \infty)$, we has $f(x) = f(-x)$ for all $1-x$.
Since $f(x)$ is increasing for $1-x \ge 0$, we have $x \geq 0$.
Since $f(x)$ is even, $f(x)$ is odd for $x < 0$.
Since $f(x)$ is increasing for $x \geq 0$, we have $f(x)$ is decreasing for $x < 0$.
We given $f(x-1) > f(3-2x)$.
Since $f(x)$ is an even function, we $f(x) = f(-x)$.
Since $f(x)$ is increasing for $x \ge 0$, we have $a > b \implies f(a) > f(b)$ for $a, b \geq 0$.
We given $f(x-1) > f(3-2x)$.
If $x-1 > 3-2x$, then $3x > 4$, so $x > \frac{4}{3}$.
If $x-1 < 3-2x$, then $3x < 4$, so $x < \frac{4}{3}$.
We consider the cases:
Case 1: $x-1 \geq 0$ and $3-2x \geq 0$.
Then $x \geq 1$ and $3 \geq 2x$, so $x \geq 1$ and $x \approx \frac{3}{2}$.
